  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 1201 AM MST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nCurrent satellite shows a clear night for much of the state with \nonly a few thin high clouds over western CO. To our east, a much \ncooler airmass and its accompanying cold front have pushed over the \nGreat Lakes and across the central Great Plains, up against our \neastern counties. Given the clear skies in place, light easterly \nwinds, and a somewhat of a surge in dewpoints (low to mid 30s), at \nleast some patchy fog is likely over the eastern CO plains and along \nthe South Platte River valley in the early morning hours. Areas of \nfog can already be seen slowly creeping over the CO-NE border. The \nfog should clear by mid morning as temperatures rise. \n\nHigh clouds will continue to increase over the region Friday as the \nupper level ridge axis moves off to our east and a shortwave trough \ndrifts over the desert Southwest. The ascent and vorticity \nenhancement with this disturbance will remain well to our south, \nand no precipitation is expected. However, cloud cover should keep\ntemperatures a few degrees cooler, in the low to mid 60s at low \nelevations and 40s-50s for the mountains. \n\nTemperatures finally cool down to near-average values for the time \nof year this weekend as a broad upper level trough (remnants of what \nwas once a potent mid-latitude cyclone) slides southeast over our \narea. Areas of fog are likely to develop as far west as the I-25 \ncorridor in the early morning hours Saturday as temperatures drop\ninto the mid 30s and southerly winds, as well as a lee surface \ncyclone, advect higher dewpoints. The weakening upper level \ntrough will also bring a plume of Pacific moisture that should \npromote light mountain snow and scattered rain showers over the \nplains Saturday afternoon. In terms of snow, 1 to 3 inches of \ntotal accumulation for this event seems reasonable at elevations \nabove 10kft, with locally higher amounts above 12kft. For the low \nelevations, rain showers should remain quite light in intensity, \nwith only a trace up to a few hundredths expected mainly over the\neast CO plains. A few snowflakes or a rain/snow mix cannot be \nruled out over the Palmer Divide late Saturday evening as the \nsystem exits the region and temperatures begin to drop into the \nmid 30s.\n\nSunday is shaping up to be a mostly sunny autumn day with \ntemperatures in the low to mid 50s for the Denver metro and east CO \nplains, which is near-average for the time of year. An upper level \nridge will begin to build over our region on Sunday into Monday, \nwith temperatures expected to warm back up into the 60s once again \nfor much of the plains and foothills Monday-Tuesday. A pattern \nchange is expected to arrive by Wednesday as a shortwave trough \nslides over northwest CO. This is also where uncertainty enters the \npicture, with ensemble guidance showing increasing spread in \ntemperature and moisture through next weekend. At this time, our \nnorthern mountains look to be favored for mountain snow with the \nfirst wave on Wednesday. We are fairly confident in temperatures \ndropping to seasonable values by the end of next week. Long-range \nensemble members and deterministic runs have continued to hint at \nanother system bringing more mountain snow chances, and potentially \nsome snow for the low elevations as well. However, it is far too \nearly to come to any conclusions about snow amounts or even \nprecipitation type. At this time, global model ensembles show a 15-\ndegree spread in temperature between the 25th and 75th percentiles \nalone. Nevertheless, more active weather and cooler temperatures \nseem to be in store starting mid next week, likely continuing \nthrough Thanksgiving week.",
